Relevant columns and how they will be used  

• `reference_name` : tells us which chromosome the variant is on.  
  – Condition: `reference_name = '17'`  

• `start`, `end` : genomic co‑ordinates.  
  – Condition: `start BETWEEN 41196311 AND 41277499` (and usually `end` in the same range).  

• `reference_bases` : the reference allele we need to list.  

• `alternate_bases` : array of alternate alleles; we will UNNEST it to get distinct values.  

• `VT` (Variant Type) : SNP / INDEL / SV etc.; required in the output.  

• `HWE` : χ² (or –log10 p) value from the Hardy‑Weinberg test that ships with 1KG.  

• `AF`, `AFR_AF`, `AMR_AF`, `ASN_AF`, `EUR_AF` : overall and super‑population allele frequencies requested in the question.  

• `AC`, `AC1`, `AN` : allele counts / allele number (optional shortcut to alt‑freq).  

• `call` (REPEATED STRUCT) → `genotype` : needed to rebuild observed numbers of  
  – homozygous reference (0/0)  
  – heterozygous (0/1 or 1/0)  
  – homozygous alternate (1/1)  

Conditions that will usually be applied  

```
WHERE reference_name = '17'
  AND start BETWEEN 41196311 AND 41277499
```

Below are 10 progressively more complex BigQuery queries (all limited to 20 rows) that examine, aggregate and finally return everything requested.  
(Replace nothing – the project, dataset and table names are already correct.)

```sql
-- 1. Description: Which chromosomes do we actually see in this slice?
SELECT DISTINCT `reference_name`
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`
WHERE start BETWEEN 41196311 AND 41277499
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Basic look at co‑ordinates and alleles in the region.
SELECT `reference_name`, `start`, `end`, `reference_bases`, `alternate_bases`
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`
WHERE reference_name = '17'
  AND start BETWEEN 41196311 AND 41277499
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Add the variant type (VT) and HWE statistic supplied by 1KG.
SELECT `reference_name`, `start`, `reference_bases`, `alternate_bases`, `VT`, `HWE`
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`
WHERE reference_name = '17'
  AND start BETWEEN 41196311 AND 41277499
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Show overall and population‑specific allele frequencies.
SELECT `reference_name`, `start`, `AF`, `AFR_AF`, `AMR_AF`, `ASN_AF`, `EUR_AF`
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`
WHERE reference_name = '17'
  AND start BETWEEN 41196311 AND 41277499
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Peek inside one variant’s genotypes (first 20 samples).
SELECT v.start, c.call_set_name, c.genotype
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
     UNNEST(v.call) AS c
WHERE v.reference_name = '17'
  AND v.start BETWEEN 41196311 AND 41277499
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Count observed genotype categories for a single variant.
SELECT v.start,
       COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] = 0 AND c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)] = 0) AS hom_ref,
       COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] != c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)])          AS het,
       COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] = 1 AND c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)] = 1) AS hom_alt
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
     UNNEST(v.call) AS c
WHERE v.reference_name = '17'
  AND v.start = 41196311          -- pick one site for illustration
GROUP BY v.start
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Same counting but for *every* variant in the interval.
SELECT v.reference_name, v.start, v.end,
       COUNT(*)                                                         AS total_gt,
       COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] = 0 AND c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)] = 0) AS hom_ref,
       COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] != c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)])          AS het,
       COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] = 1 AND c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)] = 1) AS hom_alt
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
     UNNEST(v.call) AS c
WHERE v.reference_name = '17'
  AND v.start BETWEEN 41196311 AND 41277499
GROUP BY v.reference_name, v.start, v.end
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Add expected counts under HWE using overall allele frequency (AF).
WITH obs AS (
  SELECT v.start,
         COUNT(*) AS n,
         COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] = 0 AND c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)] = 0) AS hom_ref,
         COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] != c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)])          AS het,
         COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] = 1 AND c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)] = 1) AS hom_alt,
         v.AF
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
       UNNEST(v.call) AS c
  WHERE v.reference_name = '17'
    AND v.start BETWEEN 41196311 AND 41277499
  GROUP BY v.start, v.AF
)
SELECT *,
       (1-AF)*(1-AF)*n  AS exp_hom_ref,
       2*AF*(1-AF)*n    AS exp_het,
       AF*AF*n          AS exp_hom_alt
FROM obs
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Compute a χ² value from those observed/expected numbers.
WITH obs AS (
  SELECT v.start,
         COUNT(*) AS n,
         COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] = 0 AND c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)] = 0) AS o_rr,
         COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] != c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)])          AS o_rs,
         COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] = 1 AND c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)] = 1) AS o_ss,
         v.AF AS q                              -- alt allele frequency
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` v,
       UNNEST(v.call) c
  WHERE v.reference_name = '17'
    AND v.start BETWEEN 41196311 AND 41277499
  GROUP BY v.start, v.AF
)
SELECT *,
       (1-q) * (1-q) * n  AS e_rr,
       2*q*(1-q)  * n     AS e_rs,
       q*q        * n     AS e_ss,
       ((o_rr - (1-q)*(1-q)*n)*(o_rr - (1-q)*(1-q)*n)) / NULLIF((1-q)*(1-q)*n,0) +
       ((o_rs - 2*q*(1-q)*n  )*(o_rs - 2*q*(1-q)*n  )) / NULLIF(2*q*(1-q)*n,0) +
       ((o_ss - q*q*n        )*(o_ss - q*q*n        )) / NULLIF(q*q*n,0)          AS chi_sq
FROM obs
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Final all‑in‑one query returning everything requested.
WITH genos AS (
  SELECT
    v.reference_name,
    v.start,
    v.end,
    v.reference_bases,
    (SELECT STRING_AGG(DISTINCT alt) FROM UNNEST(v.alternate_bases) alt) AS alternate_bases,
    v.VT,
    v.HWE,
    v.AF, v.AFR_AF, v.AMR_AF, v.ASN_AF, v.EUR_AF,
    COUNT(*) AS total_genotypes,
    COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] = 0 AND c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)] = 0) AS obs_hom_ref,
    COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] != c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)])          AS obs_het,
    COUNTIF(c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(0)] = 1 AND c.genotype[SAFE_OFFSET(1)] = 1) AS obs_hom_alt
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
       UNNEST(v.call) AS c
  WHERE v.reference_name = '17'
    AND v.start BETWEEN 41196311 AND 41277499
  GROUP BY v.reference_name, v.start, v.end,
           v.reference_bases, alternate_bases, v.VT,
           v.HWE, v.AF, v.AFR_AF, v.AMR_AF, v.ASN_AF, v.EUR_AF
),
stats AS (
  SELECT *,
         (1-AF)*(1-AF)*total_genotypes                     AS exp_hom_ref,
         2*AF*(1-AF)*total_genotypes                       AS exp_het,
         AF*AF*total_genotypes                             AS exp_hom_alt,
         ((obs_hom_ref - (1-AF)*(1-AF)*total_genotypes )*
          (obs_hom_ref - (1-AF)*(1-AF)*total_genotypes )) /
          NULLIF((1-AF)*(1-AF)*total_genotypes ,0) +
         ((obs_het     - 2*AF*(1-AF)*total_genotypes   )*
          (obs_het     - 2*AF*(1-AF)*total_genotypes   )) /
          NULLIF(2*AF*(1-AF)*total_genotypes ,0) +
         ((obs_hom_alt - AF*AF*total_genotypes         )*
          (obs_hom_alt - AF*AF*total_genotypes         )) /
          NULLIF(AF*AF*total_genotypes ,0)               AS chi_sq
  FROM genos
)
SELECT
  reference_name,
  start,
  end,
  reference_bases,
  alternate_bases,
  VT AS variant_type,
  chi_sq,
  HWE  AS hwe_score_from_file,
  total_genotypes,
  obs_hom_ref, exp_hom_ref,
  obs_het,      exp_het,
  obs_hom_alt,  exp_hom_alt,
  AF  AS overall_AF,
  AFR_AF, AMR_AF, ASN_AF, EUR_AF
FROM stats
ORDER BY start
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries walk from the simplest inspection of the data to a fully fledged report that lists each variant in chr 17: 41 196 311‑41 277 499 together with its alleles, type, Hardy‑Weinberg χ² statistic that we recomputed from the raw genotypes, observed/expected genotype counts, and the allele frequencies supplied by the 1000 Genomes Project.
